 ## Overview
-Hybrid Ray Tracing is a method for mixing geometric and volumetric data by casting rays through the ensemble of both, drawing samples of each, and compositing them in depth-sorted order [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering scenes containing both scalar fields (volumes) and polygonal boundaries simultaneously. Unlike pure volume rendering methods that ignore geometry or pure rasterization methods that ignore volume attenuation, Hybrid Ray Tracing explicitly handles the occlusion and attenuation interactions between the two data types [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Texture-Based Volume Rendering](4707-texture-based-volume-rendering.md) by treating polygons as distinct entities that interrupt volumetric slabs, rather than converting geometry into voxels [S1, unofficial].
+Hybrid Ray Tracing is a method for mixing geometric and volumetric data by casting rays through the ensemble of both, drawing samples of each, and compositing them in depth-sorted order [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering scenes containing both scalar fields (volumes) and polygonal surfaces simultaneously, ensuring correct visibility where polygons obscure volume and vice versa [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Texture-Based Volume Rendering](4707-texture-based-volume-rendering.md) which rasterizes proxy geometry, or [Mixed Geometry Rendering](3442-mixed-geometry-rendering.md) which may use hardware acceleration for static views, Hybrid Ray Tracing performs a unified ray-casting pass that explicitly handles the interaction between discrete polygonal intersections and continuous volumetric attenuation [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of two primary approaches: the Hybrid Ray Tracer (direct ray casting) and 3D Scan-Conversion (voxelization). The Hybrid Ray Tracer is the core algorithm described below.
